  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThis afternoon through tonight: Light Rain Showers\n\nA quick moving upper level trough is traversing southeast out of\nthe Dakotas towards the Upper Midwest this afternoon through \ntonight. This feature is propelling a surface cold front \nsoutheast that should reach the CWA tonight. Before that occurs,\nlow level temperatures will increase a few degrees today when \ncompared to yesterday so that should result in highs being a few\ndegrees warmer, but still in the 60s. Synoptic scale lift out \nahead of the trough should continue to generate isolated rain \nshowers throughout the day. The better potential for rain will \ncome later on this evening into tonight as the trough moves over\nthe region. Rain chances increase up to 40-70 percent this \nevening, with the highest chances along and north of I-94. Rain \nmay develop just south of I-90 overnight as well, but the \nchances in that occurring are much lower (10-30 percent). \nRainfall amounts should remain light around a quarter inch or \nless.\n\nSunday into Monday: Frost Potential Sunday Night/Monday Morning\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage on Sunday, the only\nnoticeable change will be the wind direction throughout the \nday. The wind should be out of the northwest with some gusts up \nto 20 mph. Afternoon high temperatures will be nearly identical \nto today in the 60s. Mostly clear skies are expected throughout \nSunday into Monday, which will aid in strong radiational cooling\novernight. Widespread lows in the lower 40s to mid 30s are \nforecast across the Upper Mississippi River Valley and frost may\ndevelop across central to western Wisconsin. The latest 03/13z \nNBM probabilities indicate a 20- 50% chance for overnight lows \nto drop down to 35 degrees or less for locations near Black \nRiver Falls, to the east, and further north towards Medford. \nFrost Advisories will likely be needed in future forecast \nupdates given this potential. After a cool start to Monday, \ntemperatures will rebound right back into the 60s with continued\nclear skies due to surface high pressure just to the south of \nthe CWA.\n\nRest of Next Week: Near Average Temperatures and Dry Conditions\n\nSynoptic flow is forecast to be mainly zonal through the next work \nweek. This pattern should lead to mainly quiet weather across much \nof the country, including the Upper Midwest. High temperatures each \nday will be around average for early October in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s. At this time, overnight lows beyond Monday morning do\nnot appear to get close to frost or freeze conditions. Mostly \nclear skies with dry conditions are forecast for much of the \nweek until mid level moisture tries to push back north late \nweek. As of now, the potential for rain associated with the \nincreased moisture appears very low, but cloud cover should \nincrease as a result. Temperatures may also rise up above \naverage late week as upper level ridging may increase over the \ncentral part of the CONUS.",
